---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const areas = [
  {
    name: 'Software Engineering',
    description: 'Modern web and desktop applications, built for performance, maintainability, security, and simplicity.',
    focus: 'TypeScript, JavaScript, React, Next.js, Bun, Python, FastAPI, Tauri.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Artificial Intelligence',
    description: 'AI-assisted development, coding agents, automation, model integrations, and practical AI applications.',
    focus: 'AI coding agents, LLM integrations, intelligent automation, AI-powered tools, OCR and computer vision.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Product Design',
    description: 'Minimal, intuitive, and visually refined digital experiences.',
    focus:
      "UI/UX, design systems, typography, visual hierarchy, responsive interfaces, interaction design. Inspired by Apple's Human Interface Guidelines, Framer, and modern minimalist interfaces.",
  },
  {
    name: 'Financial Technology',
    description: 'Experiments in market analysis, trading infrastructure, data processing, and visualization.',
    focus:
      'Market data analysis, backtesting, algorithmic trading concepts, real-time data visualization, AI-assisted analytics.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Systems & Experimentation',
    description: 'Understanding technologies beyond their surface-level functionality.',
    focus:
      'API integrations, authentication, application security, cryptography fundamentals, performance optimization, system architecture.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Behind CTLX"
  description="CTLX is the personal digital space and independent software lab of citalopram: software engineering, AI, automation, and experiments that sometimes become products."
>
  <main>
    <section class="block hero wrap">
      <div class="side"><p class="kicker">Behind CTLX</p></div>
      <div class="main">
        <h1 style="--i: 0">Independent by design.</h1>
        <p class="lead" style="--i: 1">
          CTLX is an independent software lab: a place for software engineering, AI, automation,
          and experiments that sometimes become products.
        </p>
        <div class="prose" style="--i: 2">
          <p>
            I'm an independent builder exploring the intersection of software engineering,
            artificial intelligence, and digital product design. I create tools, experiment with
            emerging technologies, and turn ideas into functional products.
          </p>
          <p>
            My interests span AI-assisted development, automation, financial technology, data
            visualization, and modern web applications.
          </p>
          <p>
            I believe good software should be fast, intuitive, and thoughtfully designed.
            Complexity should serve a purpose, not become the product.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="tags" style="--i: 3" aria-label="Identity">
          <li>Independent Developer</li>
          <li>AI Explorer</li>
          <li>Product Builder</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block wrap" id="what-i-do">
      <div class="side"><h2 class="kicker"><span>01</span>What I Do</h2></div>
      <div class="main">
        <p class="lead">Areas I work in and keep exploring. Interests, not credentials.</p>
        <ul class="list">
          {
            areas.map(({ name, description, focus }) => (
              <li>
                <span class="name">{name}</span>
                <span class="desc">
                  {description}
                  <span class="focus">{focus}</span>
                </span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block wrap" id="approach">
      <div class="side"><h2 class="kicker"><span>02</span>Approach</h2></div>
      <div class="main">
        <div class="prose">
          <p class="lead">
            Most of what I know came from taking things apart. I would rather understand how a
            tool works than only know how to use it, and I judge a technology by what it can
            actually do, whatever its popularity.
          </p>
          <p>
            I learn by building. Make something, test it, find where it breaks, go again. Each
            pass tends to remove more than it adds: the aim is to cut what a product doesn't need
            and keep everything it does.
          </p>
          <p>
            Performance, usability, and visual refinement carry equal weight. A fast tool that is
            awkward to use is unfinished, and so is a polished one that is slow. Nothing here is
            ever quite done. Existing work gets refined as practice shows what was wrong with it.
          </p>
          <p><a href="/#work">See the work</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
